C. Planning stages for the city's streets as part of the master planning stages  
Stage A - Data collection and analysis - Identification and characterization of "framework streets"  
1. Identification and definition of "framework streets":  
   - Identification of the main and significant streets for the city's function and design;  
   - Streets and roads that cross the city lengthwise and connect its parts;  
   - Active streets that generate urban activity;  
   - Streets along which important public institutions and focal points are concentrated;  
   - Boulevards, promenades, and streets with special scenic characteristics;  
   - Streets with historical and social significance;  
   - Streets with unique structural characteristics that serve as landmarks of the city;  
   - Typical streets that reflect the characteristics of most of the city's streets;  
   - Main focal points and intersections of the city's streets - squares, plazas, intersections with functional, design, or social importance.  
2. Classification and characterization of the streets:  
   - Diagnosis and definition of the functional and structural characteristics of the framework streets;  
   - Cross-section or sections characteristic of existing streets, including structural and functional characterization of the street edges;  
   - Notation of the main features of each street, as well as successes and failures in its function;  
   - Definition of the users.

