This chapter presents the planner with the main characteristics and guiding principles for intersection design. The chapter discusses the following topics:
A. Characteristics of the intersection;
B. Principles for designing the intersection in the urban space;
C. Principles for designing the intersection space;
D. Safety considerations in designing the intersection space.

Characteristics of the Intersection
The intersection as an urban "place" refers to the entire public space located between the boundaries of an "urban street." Just as the concept of a street encompasses all its components and the various activities occurring within it, the urban intersection is the shared public space of several streets along with their components and activities. 

The focal point created at the intersection as a result of the meeting of urban street spaces, its visibility and accessibility, the traffic passing through it, and the activities occurring there, make the intersection a hub for urban activity, orientation, and identification. Some city intersections have a unique urban essence and are recognized as well-known squares, important urban centers, hubs of unique urban activity, and impressive urban and architectural structures. The design of an urban intersection is the design of an important and representative urban space.

This point is important to emphasize, primarily due to the numerous limitations and constraints in intersection design stemming from functional and safety considerations. The fact that...

