2.1.3 The Intersection as a Shared Traffic Space  
The traffic characteristics at an intersection are completely different from the traffic characteristics along a street. Traffic along the street (in the segment between two intersections) generally operates as follows:  
Primarily along the street's alignment; in two basic directions;  
For each user (a lane for motorized traffic, a lane for public transport, a walking lane, a bicycle lane, etc.), or in shared lanes (vehicles and bicycles) in the same direction with small speed differences;  
Simultaneously for different street users; at different paces and speeds.  
The traffic characteristics at the intersection are completely different, and traffic operates as follows:  
Between the various arms of the intersection; in a wide variety of movement directions;  
For different movements; shared in extensive traffic areas;  
In the allocation of time between different users, and between possible movement directions at the intersection;  
Among the different street users, and within each type with smaller gaps in pace and speed compared to the street segment.  
The difference in traffic characteristics of the intersection compared to the street is significantly reflected in the number of existing conflicts.

In areas with public transportation, it is always ranked at a high level of preference.  
Safety considerations in intersection design  
The intersection is a safety vulnerability point in the public space of the city. The multitude of users and activities at the intersection requires special planning inputs for the safety of the intersection. Below are some of the important principles for achieving maximum safety at the intersection:  
Minimizing shared spaces  
It is essential to minimize the shared spaces at the intersection.  
Elimination of unnecessary traffic lanes (see section 2.3.3 above);  
Expansion of sidewalks in the intersection area (see section 5.4);  
Creation of pedestrian traffic islands where long crossings are required;  
Ensuring visibility, sightlines, and observation at the intersection  
Crossing the intersection by any user is a complex action that requires gathering and processing information, planning, preparation, and immediate decision-making. Therefore, it is important that the information available at the intersection is clear, transparent, and readable to the user for as long as possible before making decisions and executing them. All intersection users, including pedestrians, vehicle drivers, and cyclists, must be able to see and be seen from a reasonable distance before entering the shared spaces of the intersection. This principle will be achieved by planning...

