Safety barriers for pedestrians (not included in the traffic signs) are placed on the sidewalk and are intended to prevent pedestrians from descending onto the road. Safety fences are installed in the median, in places where pedestrian traffic or crossing the road poses a danger to pedestrians, or when there is a desire to direct them to a designated crossing nearby. 

The role of the barriers and safety fences is to prevent pedestrians from crossing in places where they may be harmed, endangered, or disrupt vehicle traffic. The approach to barriers and safety fences is mixed; on one hand, according to studies published worldwide, safety barriers in intersection areas reduce pedestrian accidents by 20% in the segment and by 50% on the sidewalk. On the other hand, safety barriers also have limitations, including: 
A. Interference with the visibility of drivers and pedestrians; 
B. Occupying part of the sidewalk area; 
C. Limiting pedestrian accessibility; 
D. Visual clutter; 
E. A physical obstacle that a vehicle may collide with. 

Guidelines for the installation of barriers and safety fences for pedestrians
Due to the fact that barriers and safety fences have various aspects that include pros and cons for their installation, there are no fixed justifications for their installation, and the decision should be left to the planning teams and their approvals.

The intersection design will be largely adapted to the control characteristics of the intersection. The way bicycle traffic is handled at the intersection may differ between uncontrolled intersections and those with traffic sign control, or signalized intersections where the time of use of the intersection area is allocated by the different movements.

D. Special measures for bicycle traffic at the intersection
These guidelines present various measures that can be included among the components of the intersection to integrate bicycle traffic at the intersection:
- Bicycle crossing;
- Conditional traffic light (subject to approval by the Ministry of Transport after testing);
- Special traffic lights for bicycles at crossings combined with buttons for bicycles as needed;
- Special traffic lights for bicycles crossing the intersection in a continuous lane (not at a crossing);
- Traffic signs.

To regulate bicycle traffic at the intersection and its approaches, several dedicated traffic signs are required. The signs and their usage are detailed in Chapter 6.

Traffic calming areas
In traffic calming areas, there is no separation between bicycles and other vehicles, but the Ministry of Transport's guidelines for traffic calming areas extensively address bicycle traffic at gateways (which are part of intersections), at intersections, with calming measures, and with various traffic management solutions. In planning bicycle traffic...

This is in contrast to a possible encounter with pedestrians and cars, which the driver was accustomed to in the past. To improve safety in areas where bicycles meet vehicles at exits from yards and parking lots, it is necessary to ensure an appropriate field of vision and to mark the bicycle lane in the meeting areas. The following principles are presented for regulating the encounters between entrances to yards and parking lots and the bicycle lane (at separation levels B and C):  
1. A. Keeping the edge of the bicycle lane at least 1.00 m away from the property line;  
B. Ensuring a clear field of vision when exiting the yard in both directions (if there is no field of vision, mirrors can be used);  
C. Marking a traffic sign in the meeting area;  
D. Highlighting the bicycle lane in green. This highlighting, which is recommended in all situations, is even more important in sections where there are many entrances for pedestrians and vehicles.

